# `box` and `mate` are made in front of both loops and are believed fresh where the closed copy of the loop inside begins; the typed turn that hands over to it has let them leave. The builds of that row are given
# up as a try is, and every fresh object leaves in front of them. Once the walk failed there instead: the try of the loop around took that for a failure inside it, and its own purchases were lost with it.
# RUN: compile PurchasesOfLoopsThatFailed LoopsBuiltTwice mechanisms=all
# CHECK: statistic PurchasesOfLoopsThatFailed 0
# CHECK: statistic LoopsBuiltTwice 7
# RUN(SHELL): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# SHELL: number 3 number 6 6 {"n":6} {"n":6} 3,5,5
# SHELL-NEXT: exited with 0
# SHELL-NEXT: the source prints the same
# RUN(HELD): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
